1 Overview
**********

‘psort’ reads lines from standard input or one or more files, counts the
occurrences of each unique line, and prints them sorted by frequency
from least to most common.  Each output line shows the percentage of the
total, the raw count, and the value.  A ‘Total:’ line is printed at the
end.

   It is the equivalent of:

     sort | uniq -c | sort -n

   but with cleaner formatting, aligned columns, percentages, and
additional filtering options.

     $ find Movies -type f -name '*.mp4' -o -name '*.m4v' \
         | awk -F. '{print $NF}' | psort
       2.8649%    42 mp4
      97.1351%  1424 m4v
        Total:  1466

   Four implementations are provided -- Perl, C, Go, and Python 3 -- all
supporting identical command-line options.  When installed via the RPM
or Debian package, ‘/usr/bin/psort’ is a symlink managed by
‘update-alternatives’ pointing to ‘psort_c’ by default.


