5 Implementations
*****************

Four implementations are provided, all supporting identical command-line
options:

‘psort.pl’
     The original implementation, written in Perl.  Uses a hash
     (‘%data’) for counting and Perl's built-in sort for ordering.
     Requires Perl and the ‘Getopt::Long’ module (included in standard
     Perl distributions).

‘psort_c’
     C implementation.  Uses an open-addressing hash table with FNV-1a
     hashing and dynamic resizing at a 65% load factor.  No external
     dependencies; links only against libc.

‘psort_go’
     Go implementation.  Uses Go's built-in ‘map[string]int’ for
     counting and ‘sort.Slice’ for ordering.  Compiled to a
     self-contained static binary with no runtime dependencies.

‘psort.py’
     Python 3 implementation.  Uses a ‘dict’ for counting and Python's
     built-in ‘sorted()’ for ordering.  Requires Python 3 (standard
     library only; no third-party packages).

   When installed from a package, ‘/usr/bin/psort’ is a symlink managed
by ‘update-alternatives’.  Installed priorities:

Binary                 Priority               Notes
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘psort_c’              40                     Default
‘psort_go’             30
‘psort.py’             20
‘psort.pl’             10

   To switch implementations:

     update-alternatives --config psort


