6.3 Scaling Behavior
====================

A scaling benchmark was run at input sizes from 100,000 to 650,000 lines
in 50,000-line increments, with 3 timed runs per variant per size point.
The results were plotted as time-vs-size and memory-vs-size charts
(‘scaling_real.png’, ‘scaling_user.png’, ‘scaling_sys.png’,
‘scaling_mem.png’ in the source tree).

   All four ports show broadly linear real-time growth once the correct
hash-table implementation is in place.  ‘psort_c’ is fastest at every
size point; ‘psort_go’ is second; ‘psort.py’ and ‘psort.pl’ follow, with
Perl's larger per-key hash overhead becoming more apparent at higher
line counts.

   Memory consumption scales proportionally to the number of _unique_
lines encountered.  On a dataset of file paths (high uniqueness) it
grows roughly linearly with input size across all ports.

   Before the hash table fix in ‘psort_c’ (*note The C Hash Bug::), the
scaling curve was visibly quadratic and the 650,000-line run timed out
entirely.


File: psort.info,  Node: The C Hash Bug,  Next: Observations,  Prev: Scaling,  Up: Benchmarks

6.4 The O(n^2) Bug in psort_c
=============================

The original C port used a flat array for counting: every new input line
triggered a linear scan through all previously-seen keys to find a
match.  This is O(n) per line and O(n^2) overall.

6.4.1 Symptoms
--------------

On a 107,000-line benchmark the quadratic cost was small enough (under
0.2 s) to go unnoticed.  On the 100,000-650,000-line scaling benchmark,
the growth curve was unmistakably quadratic and the 650,000-line run
timed out.  On the full 2,289,195-line input the process ran for over
four minutes before being killed.

6.4.2 Fix
---------

The fix replaced the linear scan with an open-addressing hash table:

*Hash function*
     FNV-1a (Fowler-Noll-Vo 1a) -- a simple, non-cryptographic hash
     function with good distribution for short string keys.

*Collision resolution*
     Linear probing.  Slots are probed sequentially until an empty slot
     or matching key is found.

*Load factor*
     The table is doubled when 65% of slots are occupied, keeping probe
     sequences short and lookup O(1) amortized.

*Key storage*
     Keys are heap-allocated via ‘strdup’; the hash table stores
     pointers and avoids copying strings on every lookup.

   After the fix, ‘psort_c’ went from last place (unusable at scale) to
first place on every metric: wall time, user CPU, sys CPU, and peak
memory.


File: psort.info,  Node: Observations,  Prev: The C Hash Bug,  Up: Benchmarks

6.5 Observations
================

C is fastest on every metric
----------------------------

With the hash table corrected, ‘psort_c’ leads on real time (0.519 s
average), user CPU (0.460 s), sys CPU (0.054 s), and peak memory (67.33
MB). No interpreter, no garbage collector, no runtime overhead -- just a
tight read loop and one hash lookup per line.

Go is a close second, but GC shows in sys time
----------------------------------------------

Go's 0.949 s average real time is 83% slower than C but well ahead of
the interpreted ports.  The standout cost is sys CPU (0.182 s average --
three times more than C or Python), a direct consequence of Go's
concurrent garbage collector making write-barrier and heap-scan passes
that require kernel interaction.  For a workload this memory-intensive
(2.3 million unique strings), GC pressure is the dominant cost above raw
computation.

Python beats Perl at scale and nearly matches Go
------------------------------------------------

Python (1.055 s) is only 11% behind Go and 25% faster than Perl (1.402
s).  CPython's dict scales efficiently to large key counts, and its sys
CPU (0.056 s) is essentially identical to C -- no GC churn.  Python also
uses the second-least memory (84.55 MB) -- less than Go's runtime
overhead (100.27 MB).

Perl holds last place at large scale
------------------------------------

Perl was competitive on the original 107,000-line benchmark but does not
scale as well to 2.3 million lines.  At 1.402 s it is 2.7x slower than C
and 33% slower than Python.  Perl's hash carries more per-key overhead
than Python's dict (135 MB vs.  85 MB), which compounds at large unique
key counts.


